Pantomime on OPENSTEP, and pmail
================================

Pantomime is the mail library behind GNUMail: MIME parsing and generation,
IMAP, POP3 and SMTP clients, local mbox and Maildir folders, and character set
conversion.  This package builds it for OPENSTEP 4.2 as a static library
(`/usr/local/lib/libPantomime.a`, headers in `/usr/local/include/Pantomime`)
and adds `pmail`, a command line client that fetches over IMAP or POP3 and
sends over SMTP, with TLS.

The point of the port is to have a mail engine that can talk to current mail
servers.  It is the foundation for a NeXTMail-style application; `pmail` is how
the engine is exercised until that exists.

pmail
-----

    pmail [options] command [argument ...]

    imap-folders              list the mailboxes
    imap-list [FOLDER]        list the messages in FOLDER (default INBOX)
    imap-get [FOLDER] N       print message number N (or uid:N for a UID)
    pop3-list                 list the messages in the maildrop
    pop3-get N                print message number N
    send [FILE]               send a message (FILE or standard input)

    -h HOST   -p PORT   -u USER   -m MECHANISM   -T tls|starttls|none
    -C CAFILE   -k   -f FROM   -t TO   -s SUBJECT   -w SECONDS   -d

The password comes from the environment variable `PMAIL_PASSWORD`, or is
asked for on the terminal.

    PMAIL_PASSWORD=... pmail -h imap.example.org -u me imap-list
    PMAIL_PASSWORD=... pmail -h imap.example.org -u me imap-get INBOX 3 > msg.txt
    echo "See you at 10." | pmail -h smtp.example.org -u me \
        -f me@example.org -t you@example.net -s "Tomorrow" send

Output of `imap-list` is one tab separated line per message: number, UID, date,
sender, subject.  `pop3-list` has the number and the size only, because POP3
does not give the headers without fetching the message.  `imap-get` and
`pop3-get` print the whole message with its headers; line ends are LF.

`send` with `-s` composes a plain text message (UTF-8, quoted-printable) from the
text it reads; without `-s` the input must be a complete message with
headers.  `-t` may be given more than once, and with a complete message it
overrides the recipients in the headers.

Ports default to the usual ones for the chosen security: 993, 995 and 465 for
`-T tls` (the default; TLS from the first byte), 143, 110 and 587 for
`-T starttls` and `-T none`.  Use `-T starttls` for submission on port 587.

### Authentication

IMAP uses AUTHENTICATE LOGIN by default; `-m CRAM-MD5` is also supported.  SMTP
uses PLAIN by default (`-m LOGIN` and `-m CRAM-MD5` also work).  POP3 uses
USER/PASS.

Servers that insist on OAuth 2 (Gmail and Microsoft 365 for most accounts) cannot
be used; only servers that accept a user name and password, which for those
providers means an application-specific password where they still allow one.

### TLS

TLS 1.2 or later only.  The server's certificate is checked against the trusted
authorities in `/usr/local/ssl/cert.pem` (the `ca-certificates` package) and
must name the host you connect to (in its subject alternative names, or its
common name if it has none).  The host name is sent for SNI.  If the check
fails, the connection is dropped before any password is sent and `pmail`
says why.

  * `-C FILE` trusts the authorities in FILE instead.  Use it for a server with
    its own certificate authority.
  * `-k` turns the checks off.  Do not do that over a network you do not
    control.

`-d` prints the conversation with the server on standard error.  That includes
the password (in base64 for most mechanisms), so do not paste it anywhere.

Using the library
-----------------

Pantomime is event driven: you create a `CWIMAPStore`, `CWPOP3Store` or `CWSMTP`
with a host and port, set a delegate, call `-connectInBackgroundAndNotify`
and run the run loop; the delegate hears about the connection, authentication,
folders and messages as they happen.  `pmail.m` is a complete example, and the
`Examples` directory of the Pantomime sources has two more.

For TLS from the first byte, call `-startSSL` on the service's connection in
`-connectionEstablished:`; for STARTTLS call `-startTLS` on the service in
`-serviceInitialized:` and wait for it to be called again.  Both check
certificates; `+[CWTCPConnection setCertificateVerification:]` and
`+setCAFile:` change that for the whole program, and `-sslError` on the
connection says what went wrong.

Compile with `-DCW_OPENSTEP -I/usr/local/include/Pantomime-compat` and link
with `-ObjC libPantomime.a -lssl -lcrypto -liconv -framework Foundation`; the
`-ObjC` matters, because most of the library is categories and the linker would
otherwise leave them out.

What was changed from Pantomime 1.4.0
-------------------------------------

The patch (`pantomime-1.4.0-openstep.patch`) is deliberately small in the
parts that matter, and works the same on every platform:

  * GNUstep's run loop extensions, which Pantomime used to learn when a socket
    is readable or writable, are replaced by `CWRunLoopWatcher`, which polls
    with `select()` from a 10 ms timer.  It needs nothing but Foundation.
  * DNS lookups use `gethostbyname()`.  Pantomime's own resolver parsed
    `/etc/resolv.conf`, which OPENSTEP does not use.  Lookups block.  IPv4 only.
  * TLS uses `TLS_client_method()` with a TLS 1.2 minimum, SNI, certificate
    and host name verification; the old code asked for SSLv23 and verified
    nothing.  A handshake that fails closes the connection.
  * A refused connection or unknown host is reported at once, not after the
    60 second connect timeout.
  * IMAP mailbox names are encoded and decoded with a new modified UTF-7
    (RFC 3501, 5.1.3) routine; the old one needed UTF-7 support in NSString.
  * The `Date:` header of a message is formatted by hand, in English, with the
    time zone offset.

`CWCompat.h` and `CWCompat.m`, compiled only with `-DCW_OPENSTEP`, supply what
OPENSTEP 4.2's Foundation lacks: `NSInteger`, `NSNull`, `-UTF8String`,
`-replaceOccurrencesOfString:...`, `NSValue` ranges, `-valueForKey:`,
`-replaceBytesInRange:withBytes:length:` and a few more.  Character sets that
NSString cannot convert (ISO 8859-3 to 15, KOI8-R, Big5, GB 2312, UTF-7, EUC-KR)
go through libiconv.

Not done, and not planned in this package: OAuth 2, IPv6, IMAP IDLE.

Pantomime uses POSIX `regcomp()` for threading subjects; `pmail-selftest` checks that
it works on your system.

Testing
-------

`pmail-selftest` (installed) checks MIME parsing and generation, header
encodings, character sets, mailbox name encoding and the compatibility code
without a network; `pkg test pantomime` runs it.  To try a real server set
`PMAIL_TEST_SERVER=host[:port]`, `PMAIL_TEST_USER`, `PMAIL_PASSWORD` (and
`PMAIL_TEST_CAFILE`) first.

The source tree has `linux-test/`: a script that starts Dovecot (IMAP, POP3) and a
small SMTP receiver with a throw-away certificate authority on 127.0.0.1, for
running the library and `pmail` end to end on a Linux machine.
